{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.SinCubeBounds","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.SinCubeBounds","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Bounds","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.MeanValue"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.mul_cube_cos_lt_sin_cube","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.mul_cube_cos_lt_sin_cube","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SinCubeBounds.lean#L106-L130","doc":"**`x³·cos x < sin³x` on `(0, π)`.**\n\nFor `x ≥ π/2` this is `cos x ≤ 0 < sin³x`.  For `x < π/2` it is the ladder:\n`cos x < 1 − x²/2 + x⁴/24 ≤ (1 − x²/6)³` (the last step needing only `x² ≤ 9`),\nso `x³cos x < (x − x³/6)³ < sin³x`.\n\nThis is `logSinc‴ < 0`, i.e. `logSinc″` is monotone — see the file header. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cos_lt_one_sub_sq_div_two_add_pow_four_div_24","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cos_lt_one_sub_sq_div_two_add_pow_four_div_24","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SinCubeBounds.lean#L75-L104","doc":"**`cos x < 1 − x²/2 + x⁴/24` for every `x > 0`.**\n\n`g = 1 − x²/2 + x⁴/24 − cos x` has `g(0) = 0` and `g′ = sin x − x + x³/6`, which\nis positive by the previous theorem — the two bounds bootstrap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sub_cube_div_six_lt_sin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sub_cube_div_six_lt_sin","kind":"theorem","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/SinCubeBounds.lean#L47-L73","doc":"**`sin x > x − x³/6` for every `x > 0`** — the tighter form of\n`Real.sin_gt_sub_cube`, which Mathlib states only as `x − x³/4` on `(0,1]`.\n\n`f = sin x − x + x³/6` has `f(0) = 0` and `f′ = cos x − 1 + x²/2 > 0`, the\nlatter being exactly `Real.one_sub_sq_div_two_lt_cos`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}